CSV is a format designed for humans to read on a screen; Parquet is a format designed for distributed supercomputers to process at petabyte scale.
Trying to power a modern cloud data lake with CSV files is the architectural equivalent of trying to heat a mansion with a matchbook.

Technical Breakdown :- 
The massive performance gap between the two comes down to three structural pillars:

Columnar vs. Row-Based: A CSV stores data row-by-row. If you run SELECT SUM(revenue) FROM table, 
the engine must read every single field across every single row just to find the revenue values.
Parquet stores data column-by-column. Spark can extract the isolated 'revenue' column and completely ignore the other 99 columns on disk.

Aggressive Compression: Because Parquet physically groups identical data types together (e.g., millions of integer timestamps stacked sequentially),
it achieves extreme compression using techniques like Dictionary and Run-Length Encoding. A 100GB CSV will routinely shrink to a 15GB Parquet file.

Embedded Metadata & Predicate Pushdown: CSVs are dumb text files. Parquet files are self-describing.
They embed their own strict schema and block-level statistics (like min/max values). If your query filters for year = 2026, the engine reads the Parquet metadata,
realizes a specific file only holds 2024 data, and safely skips the entire file without ever opening it.

Buisness Context :-
In cloud platforms like AWS Athena, BigQuery, or Snowflake, you are billed directly based on the volume of data stored and the megabytes of data scanned per query.
By shrinking storage footprints by 85% and allowing the compute engine to skip irrelevant columns and files, 
migrating an enterprise data lake from CSV to Parquet routinely slashes monthly cloud infrastructure bills by hundreds of thousands of dollars while making BI dashboards infinitely faster.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers often fight to keep data in CSV format at the staging layer simply because it's comfortable; they can double-click it and inspect the data in Excel.
As a data engineer, I actively remove human readability from the equation to prioritize machine efficiency.
Even if a third-party vendor drops a CSV onto our SFTP server, my very first pipeline step is to ingest it, enforce a strict schema, and write it out as a Snappy-compressed 
Parquet file in our 'Bronze' Lakehouse layer. Furthermore, Parquet natively handles deeply nested JSON-like data structures—like arrays and structs—which CSV fundamentally
cannot do without brittle string-parsing hacks.
